      blurb: 'The pirate’s armour with big shoulder boards tied on like window shutters, taken from a better man and laced back together with whatever cord was handy.\nYet what good are shutters against a dozen spear points? The Chinese general Qi Jiguang trained ordinary farmers to fight in small drilled teams, shields in front and long bamboo spears behind. At Taizhou in 1561 the pirates fell by the hundreds. Cheap bamboo, used cleverly, beat stolen armour.\n“A pirate in borrowed armour, or a corpse in borrowed armour. It all depended on whether the farmers held their drill.”',
