// What each one-set outfit is, for the picker.
export const OUTFIT_BLURBS = {
  boxing: 'Trunks, laced boots and padded leather gloves: the boxing ring after the Queensberry rules of 1867 made the sport respectable enough for gentlemen to bet on. The gloves were sold as the safety upgrade.\nYet whom did the gloves really protect? They saved the hands, so boxers could punch harder and more often. The brain paid the difference. The promoters kept the purse, and old champions shuffled and slurred through their last years.\n“A champion’s belt, or a punch-drunk shuffle. It all depended on how many rounds the promoter sold.”',
  judogi: 'The judogi: a jacket of heavy double-woven cotton, made thick so that it can be seized by the collar and sleeve without tearing, and a belt that says how long he has been thrown. Kano Jigoro took it from the jujutsu of samurai retainers in 1882 and called it the gentle way: the art of using another man\'s weight against him.\nYet the gentle way went where it was sent. Imperial Japan taught it to police and conscripts, to men who would take Nanjing and Manila, and after the surrender the victors made it an Olympic sport. The jacket was the same thickness throughout; only the grip on it changed hands.\n“A gentle way, or a drill for the bayonet. It all depended on who was holding the collar.”',
  dobok: 'The dobok of taekwondo: a white cotton jacket and trousers, a coloured belt tied twice, bare feet on the mat. Born in South Korea after the Korean War and drilled into army conscripts — very clean, very orderly, very white.\nYet where did all that white cotton end up? In the 1960s Korean instructors taught the art to soldiers bound for the Vietnam War, where nobody fought with their feet for long. The belt measured years of training. The war measured something else.\n“A black belt, or a body bag. It all depended on which unit his draft notice named.”',
  mma: 'Fight shorts and thin fingerless gloves: the uniform of the cage. Mixed martial artists learned every style and trusted none of them — the wrestler’s grip, the boxer’s jab, the choke artist’s patience.\nYet who got rich off ‘no rules’? The first tournaments of the 1990s sold exactly that. The fighters got small purses and the whole risk; the company that bought the league later sold it for billions.\n“A pioneer of the sport, or a forgotten name on a fight card. It all depended on who owned the broadcast rights.”',
  sports: 'Running kit of light synthetic fabric, bright and sweat-wicking, with foam-cushioned shoes for the long road. The whole outfit weighs less than a loaf of bread. It is built for speed and absolutely nothing else.\nYet whose road is it, really? The roads were built for two-tonne cars, and he is only borrowing the edge. His jacket’s reflective strips are the whole of his armour against a century of engineering, and they did their job perfectly. They caught the headlights.\n“A personal best, or a roadside memorial. It all depended on whether he took out one earbud at the crossing.”',
  sumo: 'The mawashi alone: a band of heavy silk, metres long, wrapped tight around the hips for the opponent to grab and throw by. Sumo wrestlers build their bodies for one thing, the push — a man turned into his own mountain.\nYet who pays for the mountain? In the training stables, young wrestlers are force-fed and beaten into bulk by their seniors, and some have died of it. Retired wrestlers carry the weight into hearts and knees that give out decades early.\n“A grand champion, or a broken body at forty. It all depended on how cruel his stablemaster was.”',
  business: 'A wool suit, leather-soled shoes and a silk tie knotted at the throat: the armour of the office. It is cut to show rank and nothing more, worn by a man who only ever planned to fight in meetings.\nYet what does a suit protect when the market crashes? Generations wore it to the trading floor, and in the crash of 1929, the story goes, some wore it out of the window. The suit was perfectly pressed. The accounts were not.\n“A corner office, or a ledge on the fourteenth floor. It all depended on the stock ticker that Tuesday.”',
  casual: 'A flannel shirt and chinos: clothes for an ordinary day — an errand, a coffee, a walk to the car. Soft cotton worn thin at the cuffs, chosen for comfort, by a man who expected absolutely nothing to happen.\nYet how many news stories start exactly like this? The last photo always shows someone dressed just so, smiling at something off camera, with no idea the day had other plans.\n“An ordinary afternoon, or the photo on the evening news. It all depended on which car park he chose.”',
  hiking: 'A waterproof shell jacket and stout leather boots with deep-tread soles, made for wind on a mountain ridge. It keeps out the rain, holds in the heat, and grips loose rock like a goat — a very confident outfit.\nYet who keeps count on the mountain? Rescue teams do. Every year they find walkers in kit just like this, at the bottom of a slope in spring, or never at all — a bright jacket fading on a ledge nobody can reach.\n“A summit selfie, or a search party. It all depended on whether the snow ledge held his weight.”',
  thug: 'A track jacket, a beanie pulled low, trainers scuffed at the toes: the uniform of the street corner, cheap and easy to throw away.\nYet who raised him to it? The closed youth club, the jobs that moved abroad. The knife he carries is meant to earn respect, and usually ends up in a friend, or himself, in a stairwell that smells of rain.\n“A name on the corner, or a name on a wall of flowers. It all depended on who backed down first in the stairwell.”',
  yakuza: 'His back bared to show the irezumi: a lifetime of hand-pricked tattoos, stopping at wrists and collar so a business shirt can hide them. A membership card, printed in skin.\nYet what does membership cost? He strips to fight so the enemy sees whom he belongs to. When he lets his boss down, he cuts off the tip of his little finger and hands it over, wrapped in cloth, with a polite bow. The boss keeps all ten.\n“A sworn brother, or a man short a finger. It all depended on whether his boss accepted the apology.”',
  victorianGent: 'A wool frock coat, a starched collar and a silk top hat: a Victorian gentleman of the 1860s. Fit for the club, the church and the argument afterwards — respectability, buttoned to the neck.\nYet whose dirty hands kept his so clean? His dividends came from cotton mills and the opium trade, worked by people, many of them children, he never had to meet. The coat strolled through the slums without collecting a speck.\n“A pillar of society, or a body in the Thames. It all depended on which street he took home from the club.”',
  victorianLady: 'A whalebone corset laced tight, layers of petticoats and a hooped crinoline: a Victorian lady in full dress. The corset holds her stiff as a breastplate, and the skirts drag at every step — fashion as a very elegant cage.\nYet what happens when a cage meets a fireplace? Crinolines caught fire so often that thousands of women burned to death in them. Oscar Wilde lost two half-sisters that way in 1871.\n“The belle of the ball, or a torch on the hearthrug. It all depended on how close she stood to the fire.”',
  rags: 'A torn shirt and bare feet, the cloth gone grey from being slept in. The beggar, the prisoner, the forgotten man, who sold his last belongings for bread — down to nothing but his own stubbornness.\nYet what has every age done with men like him? The workhouse, the debtors’ prison, the ship to the penal colony in Australia. Society has always had a tidy way of getting rid of him, and he has always, somehow, outlasted it for a while.\n“A survivor, or a pauper’s grave. It all depended on what the magistrate had for breakfast.”',
  commoner: 'A wool tunic and hose, a hood and a belt for a knife: a medieval farm labourer. He grew the food that fed the knights, who then rode over his fields — he paid for the armour and never got to wear any.\nYet what is a king’s promise worth? In 1381 English peasants rose under Wat Tyler and marched on London over a new tax. The teenage King Richard II promised them everything, Tyler was stabbed, and every promise was broken by harvest.\n“A free man, or a hanged rebel. It all depended on whether the king remembered his promises.”',
  joan: 'Bright white plate from head to foot under a red tunic, made in 1429 at the future king’s expense for a farm girl from Domrémy. Joan of Arc carried a banner, not a sword, so she would never have to kill.\nYet what did the king she crowned do for her? She won Orléans and crowned Charles. When she was captured, he left her to the English, who burned her as a witch in 1431. She was nineteen. He did nothing.\n“A saint in white, or a witch at the stake. It all depended on which bishop sat in judgement.”',
  templar: 'Mail from head to foot under a white surcoat with a red cross, and a flat-topped great helm: the Knights Templar, monks sworn to poverty, dressed like an army of angels.\nYet how did monks sworn to poverty become bankers to kings? They grew rich and lent everywhere. King Philip of France, deep in their debt, had them all arrested on Friday the thirteenth in 1307 and burned their leader. Debt cleared.\n“A soldier of Christ, or a heretic on the bonfire. It all depended on how much the King of France owed him.”',
  tournament: 'The Stechzeug, a German jousting armour bolted to a thick frog-mouthed helmet. The rider sees the lance only by lifting his head at the last second. Armourers built their best work for the rich man’s favourite sport.\nYet who remembers that games can kill? In 1559 King Henry II of France jousted at his daughter’s wedding. His opponent’s lance shattered, and a splinter went through the visor into his eye. He took ten days to die.\n“A champion of the lists, or a king with a splinter in his eye. It all depended on where the lance broke.”',
  police: 'A navy uniform with a soft bulletproof vest of woven fibre, and a belt carrying radio, cuffs and pistol. Enough to stop a knife or a handgun bullet on an ordinary shift.\nYet what if the shift is not ordinary? In North Hollywood in 1997 two bank robbers in homemade armour fired rifles for forty-four minutes while police bullets bounced off. Officers borrowed rifles from a gun shop. Afterwards police everywhere bought rifles, and contractors were glad to help.\n“A routine call, or a war zone. It all depended on what the robbers had in the boot.”',
  riot: 'Riot gear: padded suit, hard plastic shells on the limbs, a helmet with a clear visor, a long shield and a baton. A whole industry sells it, and business is good every time the public is angry.\nYet which side is the villain? On both sides stand people who mostly did not choose to be there. The same kit has protected crowds and charged them, and history decides later, rarely kindly, who wore the bad guy’s costume.\n“A keeper of the peace, or the face on the front page. It all depended on which way the camera was pointing.”',
  swat: 'Heavy tactical armour: hard plates, a bulletproof helmet with a sealed visor, and a ram for the door. Born in 1960s Los Angeles after a sniper showed how outgunned police were, then sold to every small-town force with a grant to spend.\nIt has saved hostages who would otherwise be dead. Yet what if the address is wrong? Sometimes the warrant names the wrong house, the man inside grabs whatever is near, and nobody goes home the same.\n“A rescuer, or a home invader with a badge. It all depended on one digit of the house number on the warrant.”',
  infantry: 'A modern soldier: ceramic plates front and back, an aramid helmet, a backpack heavier than both. Defence contractors billed a fortune for it, and the plates genuinely stopped rifle bullets that would have killed his grandfather.\nYet what does a chest plate know about the road underneath? In Iraq and Afghanistan the bombs came from below, where no plate was. A generation came home with whole chests and missing legs, and the contractors sold the army new trucks.\n“A homecoming, or a folded flag. It all depended on which stretch of road the convoy took.”',
  specialForces: 'A special-forces operator: plate carrier, cut-down helmet, night-vision goggles, everything stripped for speed. The newest gear goes to these few first, years before the regular army sees it.\nYet what if the plan runs long? In Mogadishu in 1993 US Rangers left their back plates and night-vision behind for what was meant to be an hour’s raid. It turned into a night and a morning of street fighting, and eighteen Americans did not come back.\n“A quiet in-and-out, or a city at war. It all depended on whether the helicopters stayed in the air.”',
  proximity: 'A firefighter’s silver proximity suit, reflecting the heat of burning fuel, its gold-tinted visor a mirror. Space-age materials, paid for by airlines and oil companies who would rather not lose a plane or a refinery.\nThe insurers wanted the assets saved, and the suit saves people too. At airports, crews walk toward burning jets, close enough to drag a pilot out. One step nearer and the suit stops promising anything.\n“A rescuer in silver, or a mirror that melted. It all depended on one step more, or one step less.”',
  fireEntry: 'A fire-entry suit: thick silver layers and insulation, worn to walk into the flames, with air from a tank on the back. It buys a few minutes from the fire, and the wearer counts every one.\nYet what about the fire you cannot see? At Chernobyl in 1986 the first firemen climbed onto the burning reactor in ordinary gear, because officials insisted it was a simple roof fire. Within weeks most of them were dead from radiation.\n“A hero of the night shift, or a name on a lead coffin. It all depended on whether anyone told him the truth.”',
  humanoid: 'A full-size humanoid robot: an aluminium frame in white plastic shells, motors humming like trapped insects. Built by tech firms racing for a trillion-dollar market, to carry our boxes and, one day, do our jobs.\nIt will lift what breaks human backs, and work the shifts nobody wants. Yet who gets the wages it saves? Not the warehouse worker it replaces. Its makers filmed engineers kicking it to show off its balance; the investors loved it.\n“A helper in the warehouse, or the reason for the layoffs. It all depended on who owned the robot.”',
  miniHumanoid: 'A small humanoid robot, knee-high, metal and servos with a camera for a head. Built to teach children, dance at trade shows and play robot football — the friendly demo that keeps the research money coming.\nYet what happens to last year’s model? Every tournament its kind topple, twitch and get carried off by grown men in lanyards. Every year the new one is faster, and the old ones go into a box marked spares.\n“A champion on the pitch, or a box of spare parts. It all depended on how charged its battery was at kickoff.”',
  combatBot: 'An armoured combat robot, a rifle and a shotgun for forearms, sensors for a head, a model number for a name. A defence contractor’s dream product: a soldier that never needs a pension.\nIt can go where a human would die, and that is real. Yet who answers when a machine kills? In Dallas in 2016 police sent a bomb-disposal robot with explosives toward a cornered gunman. It was the first time US police killed with a robot.\n“A life-saving tool, or an executioner by remote control. It all depended on who held the controller.”',
  exoArmoured: 'A soldier sealed in armour over a powered exoskeleton, motors carrying weight his body never could. Every punch carries more than his own strength — a man upgraded to a forklift.\nYet what did the money buy? The US Army spent years and a fortune on a suit called TALOS. The batteries lasted hours, the contract lasted longer, and the project was quietly cancelled in 2019. Pieces of it now help factory workers lift without wrecking their backs.\n“An iron titan, or a very expensive statue. It all depended on how much charge was left in the battery.”',
  silverback: 'A silverback gorilla, leader of his family, his back gone grey with age. He needs no armour; his arms could fold a man in half — but he would honestly rather eat leaves.\nYet who was the real danger in the forest? Humans hunted his kind for trophies and sold their hands to tourists as ashtrays. In 1977 poachers killed Digit, a gorilla the researcher Dian Fossey loved, and in 1985 someone killed Fossey too.\n“The king of the mountain, or an ashtray in a souvenir shop. It all depended on whether the poachers found his family first.”',
  monkeyFur: 'A wild primate — a macaque, a baboon or a chimpanzee: fur, muscle, long teeth, gripping hands and a cunning older than ours, in a face uncomfortably like our own.\nYet what did we do with the resemblance? We strapped his relatives into rockets and lab equipment, and some of our medicine owes them. In 2009 a pet chimpanzee in Connecticut, raised as a child of the house, savagely attacked a visitor and was shot.\n“A pampered member of the family, or an animal shot in the kitchen. It all depended on how long we pretended he was one of us.”',
  kungfu: 'A loose silk suit with knotted cloth buttons, matching trousers and cloth shoes: the dress of the kung fu masters of southern China, teaching in courtyards among the incense smoke. Discipline, worn as lightly as breath.\nYet what is faith against a rifle? In 1900 the Boxer rebels, trained in martial arts, believed their rituals made them bulletproof. They rose against foreigners in China, eight foreign armies marched on Beijing, and the rifles settled the debate.\n“A master of the courtyard, or a believer in the firing line. It all depended on whether the magic actually worked against bullets.”',
  monk: 'The grey cotton robe of a Shaolin monk, leggings bound at the shins, head shaved. The monks made fighting a spiritual practice — a prayer said with the fists.\nYet what protects a temple from the rulers it served? In 621, the story goes, thirteen monks helped a future Tang emperor win a war, and the temple was rewarded with land. In 1928 a warlord burned it, library and all, for forty days.\n“A temple’s guardian, or ash on the mountain. It all depended on which warlord controlled the province that year.”',
  wuxia: 'The robe and sash of a wandering swordsman from Chinese adventure tales, a cloth-wrapped sword on his back. He answers to no lord, only his own code of honour — exactly the sort of man governments love.\nCan a government tolerate a man the people like more? The historian Sima Qian wrote of real knights-errant around 100 BC. The emperor had the most famous, Guo Xie, executed with his whole family, for being too popular.\n“A folk hero, or an outlaw on the chopping block. It all depended on how loudly the people sang his name.”',
  wokou: 'A pirate of the Chinese coast in the 1500s, in a loose robe, a long blade in his sash. Japanese, Chinese and Portuguese all sailed under the name, because the Ming emperors had banned trade by sea.\nWho made the pirates? The ban did, turning merchants into smugglers and smugglers into raiders. When the court lifted it in 1567 the raids faded — after whole coastal districts had burned, which the court called a victory.\n“A merchant, or a pirate. It all depended on which emperor’s trade ban was in force that year.”',
  guanYu: 'Guan Yu, famous general of the Three Kingdoms, in a green robe over gilded scale armour, his long beard carefully combed. He swore brotherhood with the warlord Liu Bei, the legend says — loyalty, made flesh and steel.\nYet what is loyalty worth when your ally switches sides? He held a key province and lost it when a supposed ally attacked from behind. He was beheaded in 220, and his head was posted to a rival as a gift.\n“A living general, or a god in a thousand temples. It all depended on whether his allies kept their word.”',
  hanSoldier: 'A Han soldier: iron plate over a red robe, a helmet of narrow iron strips and a crossbow — a mass-produced trigger mechanism, centuries ahead of Europe. One small part of the machine that manned the Silk Road’s signal towers.\nYet what survives of him? His paperwork. Bamboo records in the desert list his requests for boots, reports of deserters, and the names of the sick and the dead, all in a clerk’s neat hand.\n“A veteran home from the border, or a line in a clerk’s ledger. It all depended on whether the signal fire was lit in time.”',
  hanCavalry: 'A Han horseman in iron plate cut for riding, a ring-handled sword at his hip. Emperor Wu bred bigger horses and forged more iron to send riders like him deep into the steppe after the nomads.\nYet what waited for him back home? Bureaucrats. In 119 BC the veteran general Li Guang got lost in the desert and arrived late for a battle. Rather than face the court’s clerks, he cut his own throat.\n“A hero of the steppe, or a man before the court clerks. It all depended on whether his guide knew the desert.”',
  bannerArmour: 'The Eight Banners’ armour: the Ming brigandine’s own make, iron plates riveted inside a long studded coat, in one of only eight colours — yellow, white, red and blue, plain or bordered. Nurhaci sorted every Jurchen household into a banner, and the banner fed, armed and drilled it. The coat was copied from the enemy; the men inside it were not.\nYet who filled the banner’s granaries? At Fushun in 1618 Nurhaci took the town, three hundred thousand head of captured people and livestock, and handed the captives out to the banners as bondservants. Every victory paid for the next coat, and the bill went to whoever had lost.\n“A soldier of the banner, or a slave of it. It all depended on which side of the wall he was born.”',
  bayara: 'The Bayara, the banners’ guard: brocade over plates, a steel mirror over the heart, gilt on the shoulders and steel on the arms, the first horsemen into any gap. Picked from every company, rewarded first from every sack — the same coat of plates as the Ming general’s household men, only better paid on time.\nSo what made them so loyal? Plunder, shared out by rank, the guard’s share counted before the rest. In 1619 they rode down four Ming columns one at a time; the silver that should have paid the Ming garrisons had been sitting in Beijing’s eunuch-run treasuries for years.\n“The Khan’s right hand, or the last man paid. It all depended on whose treasury was empty.”',
  ironPagoda: 'The Iron Pagoda, heavy cavalry of the Jin: iron plates from neck to shin, a heavy helmet, armoured horses. A moving wall of iron rolling south against the Song.\nYet what is a wall with legs? At Yancheng in 1140 the Song general Yue Fei sent men underneath with axes to cut the horses’ legs, and it worked. Two years later his own government executed him on made-up charges, to please the enemy.\n“An unstoppable wall, or a heap of lame horses. It all depended on whether the foot soldiers dared crawl under the hooves.”',
  mingGarrison: 'A Ming garrison soldier: a hemp vest, a sash, a cloth tied round the head and a spear from a damp storeroom. He was a soldier by birth, because his father was, and his father before him — inherited like a field.\nYet who took the field? His officers did, along with his food rations. By the late Ming, men deserted by the hundreds of thousands. Those who stayed worked their officers’ farms and only practised drills when inspectors were due.\n“A soldier of the empire, or his officer’s unpaid farmhand. It all depended on when the inspectors were coming.”',
  mingIronLamellar: 'Ming border cavalry: iron plates over a Mongol-style coat, a pointed steppe helmet, a horse bow. They dressed like the enemy and were often the enemy’s cousins — a Great Wall built partly of borrowed loyalty.\nYet who guards the gate when the pay is late? Whole garrisons switched sides with the seasons. In 1644 general Wu Sangui opened the Wall’s main gate to the Manchus, who rode in and took the empire he was paid to protect.\n“A guardian of the Wall, or a turncoat at the gate. It all depended on which side last paid the garrison.”',
  ashigaru: 'A Japanese foot soldier: black-lacquered body armour of riveted iron strips and a cone-shaped hat with his lord’s crest. All of it mass-produced and lent from the castle store, with a spear or a musket — war on an industrial scale, staffed by farmers.\nWho counted the sets that never came back? At Sekigahara in 1600 thousands of nameless heads were buried in mounds, and the armour was collected and lent to the next man.\n“A soldier on loan, or a head in the burial mound. It all depended on which way the turncoat general turned at Sekigahara.”',
  oyoroi: 'The great early samurai armour in full: silk-laced plates, broad shoulder boards, a helmet with gilded horns. So heavy the noble archer inside could barely walk — luckily he owned a horse for that.\nYet what was inside it? At Ichi-no-Tani in 1184 a veteran knocked a fleeing enemy from his horse, pulled off the helmet, and found a boy of sixteen with a flute in his belt. He killed him anyway, wept, and became a monk.\n“A warrior’s glory, or a boy’s last breath. It all depended on whether the boy turned back to fight.”',
  ronin: 'A masterless samurai: a worn cloak over odd pieces of armour, a straw hat pulled low, and two swords he cannot bring himself to sell. His salary died with his lord.\nWhat does loyalty to a dead man cost? In 1703 forty-seven such men plotted for two years, killed their lord’s enemy in the snow, and laid the head on his grave. The government praised their loyalty, then ordered them all to kill themselves.\n“A legend of the stage, or a beggar on the road. It all depended on whether he kept his oath for two long years.”',
  sohei: 'A Buddhist warrior monk: a white hood wrapped up to the eyes, robes over lamellar armour, and a naginata as long as a doorway. For centuries they marched on Kyoto carrying holy shrines to bully the imperial court.\nYet who could control them? One emperor famously listed three things he could not command: the river, the dice, and the monks. The warlord Oda Nobunaga could. In 1571 he burned their mountain temple and killed its monks, women and children.\n“A holy terror, or a martyr in the flames. It all depended on whether the next warlord feared the gods.”',
  hoplomachus: 'The hoplomachus gladiator: a crested, brimmed helmet with a face grille, padded leggings and tall shin guards, a small round shield, a spear and a dagger for when the spear was gone. Dressed as a Greek soldier, for a Roman crowd.\nYet what happened to the real Greek heroes? Rome turned them into a costume, worn by slaves and prisoners. Gladiator gravestones, when they had any, list fights won and age at death — rarely over thirty.\n“The crowd’s favourite, or dust under the sand. It all depended on whether the crowd waved for mercy.”',
  murmillo: 'The murmillo gladiator: a broad-brimmed helmet with a fish-shaped crest and a face grille, a padded sleeve on the sword arm, one shin guard, and a big curved shield. Named, it is thought, after a kind of fish.\nYet what does a fisherman want with a fish? His usual opponent, the net-fighter, would sing: ‘I’m not after you, I’m after your fish.’ In a gladiator graveyard in Turkey lie skulls with three neat holes, exactly the width of a trident.\n“A champion of the arena, or three holes in a skull. It all depended on whether the net fell short.”',
  retiarius: 'The retiarius gladiator: no helmet, a metal shoulder guard, a padded sleeve, a weighted net and a trident. The arena’s fisherman, quick and nearly naked, his uncovered face considered a bit shameful.\nYet why would a nobleman choose it? The poet Juvenal mocked a Roman aristocrat from an ancient family who fought as a retiarius, precisely so all Rome could see his face — and watch him run away.\n“A nobleman’s hobby, or a public disgrace. It all depended on whether the crowd recognised his face.”',
  scissor: 'The scissor gladiator: a steel tube over the left forearm ending in a curved blade, scale armour on the body, and a smooth egg-shaped helmet with two round eyeholes. Known from a few carvings and very little else.\nYet why is he so forgotten? Historians still argue about his name and what he was for. Whoever wore the tube had his name carved once, briefly, and the rest of him went into the sand without a single written record.\n“A star of the games, or a name nobody can read. It all depended on whether anyone paid a stonecutter.”',
  secutor: 'The secutor gladiator: a smooth, egg-shaped helmet with two tiny eyeholes so a trident could not get in, a padded sleeve and a big shield. His job was to chase the net-fighter around the arena.\nYet what if the chase goes on too long? The eyeholes were so small that he was soon breathing his own stale air. The net-fighter only had to keep running and wait, and the helmet would do the strangling for him.\n“The hunter, or a man suffocating in his own helmet. It all depended on how quickly he cornered the net-man.”',
  thraex: 'The Thracian gladiator: a griffin-crested helmet with a face grille, tall shin guards, a small square shield and a curved short sword. Rome dressed him up as one of its defeated enemies, for fun.\nYet what if the costume fights back? Spartacus, the story goes, broke out of gladiator school in 73 BC with seventy men and kitchen knives. Two years later six thousand of his followers were crucified along the road to Rome.\n“A free man, or a body on a cross. It all depended on whether the school kitchen was locked.”',
  maximus: 'A Roman general turned gladiator: a leather breastplate, bracers on the forearms, no helmet, and a sword scarred from wars in the north. Once he commanded armies; now he waits for the crowd’s thumb.\nYet how does a man of rank fall so far? Rome had real ones — noblemen ruined by debt or politics who signed themselves over to a gladiator school, swearing to accept fire, chains, whip and sword. The Senate banned it, then had to ban it again.\n“A general reborn, or a slave in fine leather. It all depended on how deep his debts ran.”',
  lorarius: 'The lorarius, whip-man of the arena, in a plain tunic with a whip at his belt. His job was to drive reluctant fighters onto the sand — middle management of the slaughter.\nYet what did he see each afternoon? When a fighter fell and stayed down, attendants dressed as gods of the dead tested him with a hot iron and dragged him out through the Gate of Death. The whip-man watched every one.\n“A spectator with a whip, or the next name on the bill. It all depended on whether the school was short of fighters that day.”',
  commodus: 'The emperor Commodus as Hercules, a lion skin over his head and a club in his hand. Son of the philosopher-emperor Marcus Aurelius, he fought in the arena against men who knew better than to win — and billed the treasury for every fight.\nWho laughs at an emperor? He cut the heads off ostriches and waved them at senators, who chewed leaves to keep from laughing. In 192 his own wrestling coach strangled him in his bath.\n“A living god, or a strangled fool. It all depended on whom he trusted in the bath.”',
  legionary: 'A Roman legionary: armour of iron bands on leather straps, a curved shield, a short sword and a steel helmet. Standardised kit from state workshops — an army dressed by a supply chain.\nYet what if your guide is a traitor? In AD 9 the general Varus led three legions into a German forest, guided by Arminius, a local chief he trusted as a friend. It was an ambush. All three legions died in the rain and mud.\n“A conqueror of Germany, or rust in a bog. It all depended on whether Varus listened to the warning about his friend.”',
  republicanLegionary: 'A Roman legionary of the Republic: a mail shirt, a bronze helmet with a knob on top, an oval shield and two throwing spears. A farmer who paid for his own kit, called up for one season and kept for years.\nWhat does Rome do with its survivors? At Cannae in 216 BC Hannibal surrounded eight legions and killed some fifty thousand men in an afternoon. The generals went home; the survivors were exiled to Sicily in disgrace for years.\n“A citizen hero, or an exile in Sicily. It all depended on where he stood in the line at Cannae.”',
  centurion: 'A Roman centurion: a mail shirt hung with medals, shin guards, a sideways crest on his helmet so his men could find him, and a vine stick for beating them. Authority, worn as plainly as a scar.\nYet who hated the stick most? The men on the other end of it. In a mutiny in AD 14, soldiers murdered a centurion they nicknamed ‘Bring Me Another’, after the vine sticks he kept breaking over their backs.\n“A decorated hero, or a murdered tyrant. It all depended on how many sticks he broke.”',
  lateRoman: 'A late Roman soldier: a mail shirt and a cheap helmet of two iron halves joined along the top, silvered for officers. Made in a hurry in state factories — quantity over quality.\nWho turned the refugees into enemies? In 376 Rome let the Goths cross the Danube, then its officials starved them and sold them dog meat. At Adrianople two years later the Goths destroyed the eastern army, and the emperor with it.\n“A defender of the border, or one more body at Adrianople. It all depended on whether the emperor waited for reinforcements.”',
  romanAuxiliary: 'A Roman auxiliary cavalryman: a mail shirt, a helmet with broad cheek guards, an oval shield and a long sword. Not a Roman citizen, but a German or a Thracian who would become one after twenty-five years of service.\nYet what if the deal turns sour? The Batavians were Rome’s best horsemen. In AD 69 they rebelled under Julius Civilis, one of their own and a veteran Roman officer, and burned the very forts they had been guarding.\n“A Roman citizen at last, or a rebel with a torch. It all depended on how badly Rome treated his people.”',
  goth: 'A Visigoth warrior: a wool tunic and trousers, a round shield with an iron centre, a spear and no armour. His people crossed into the Roman Empire in 376, fleeing the Huns and begging for land.\nYet what can a people with nothing do? Quite a lot, it turns out. In 410, under their king Alaric, they captured and looted Rome itself, the first enemy inside its walls in eight hundred years.\n“A refugee, or the conqueror of Rome. It all depended on whether Rome fed them when they first arrived.”',
  gothNoble: 'A Visigoth noble: a mail shirt, a riveted iron helmet, a gold-hilted sword and a garnet eagle brooch. His people carved a kingdom out of Roman Spain and ran it for three centuries, mostly by arguing over who should be king.\nWhat ends a kingdom that old? A grudge. In 711 King Roderic rode against a small army from North Africa. His rivals deserted him mid-battle, and the whole kingdom fell in a single summer.\n“A king of Spain, or a lord without a kingdom. It all depended on whether his rivals stayed on the field.”',
  gaziAlp: 'One of the first Ottoman frontier warriors: a mail shirt under a padded coat, a pointed helmet, a sabre and a horse bow. Small bands of raiders like him carved a state out of the crumbling Byzantine Empire.\nYet how do you keep an empire in the family? By killing the rest of the family. The Ottoman sultans made a habit of executing their own brothers. When Mehmed III took the throne in 1595, nineteen of his brothers were strangled in a single day.\n“A sultan, or a strangled brother. It all depended on which son reached the capital first.”',
  janissary: 'A Janissary: a long coat and a tall white felt hat, no armour. Taken from a Christian family as a boy and raised to serve the Sultan alone, he was among Europe’s first troops drilled with guns — a slave turned backbone of the empire.\nWhat happens when the backbone has opinions? To topple a Sultan, they overturned their soup pots. In 1826 the Sultan shelled their barracks and called it the Auspicious Incident.\n“A kingmaker, or ashes in the barracks. It all depended on whether the soup pots went over before the cannons were loaded.”',
  azap: 'An Ottoman azap, a foot archer or marine: a plain tunic, a turban over a felt cap, a bow and a short sword, and no armour. A farmer who volunteered for one campaign and was paid by the day.\nYet who gets sent in first? At the siege of Constantinople in 1453 men like him went first in the final assault, to tire out the defenders and fill the ditch with their bodies. Then the elite troops walked in over them.\n“A day’s wages, or a body in the ditch. It all depended on which wave he was ordered into.”',
  saladin: 'Saladin, Sultan of Egypt and Syria: a silk coat with mail sewn inside, and a tall pointed helmet. A Kurdish soldier who united Cairo and Damascus under one flag — and wore silk on the outside to do it.\nCan a merciful man also be merciless? At Hattin in 1187 he cut the crusaders off from water in July. He had the captured warrior monks beheaded. Then he took Jerusalem and spared its people.\n“A gracious victor, or an executioner. It all depended on which vows the prisoner had taken.”',
  cataphract: 'A Parthian heavy cavalryman: scale armour head to foot, his horse armoured too, a long lance in both hands. Only the nobility could afford the iron and the giant horses — a hammer of iron, owned by the few.\nWhat is a hammer without its arrows? At Carrhae in 53 BC Crassus, the richest man in Rome, lost his army to these riders and their archers. Legend says the Parthians poured molten gold down his throat to finally satisfy his greed.\n“An iron hammer, or a sitting target. It all depended on whether the archers kept the Romans pinned down.”',
  parthianArcher: 'A Parthian horse archer: tunic, trousers, a soft cap and a bow of horn and sinew. He could shoot backwards while riding away — the original Parthian shot.\nWhat is patience against a supply chain? At Carrhae in 53 BC the Romans locked shields and waited for the arrows to run out. Then a thousand camels arrived carrying more. Logistics, not courage, won the day.\n“A ghost on the wind, or a rider caught by the legion. It all depended on whether the camels showed up with more arrows.”',
  hun: 'A Hun horseman: a long coat, a fur cap, goatskin leggings, a bow and a lasso. A Roman writer claimed they ate and slept on horseback, and their powerful recurved bows outranged anything Rome had.\nWhat holds a horde together? One man. Attila extorted cartloads of gold from Rome, then in 453 died on his wedding night, choking on a nosebleed. Within a few years his empire had fallen apart.\n“The scourge of Rome, or a rider with no one to follow. It all depended on Attila surviving his wedding night.”',
  steppeLight: 'A Mongol light horseman: a silk-belted robe, a fur hat, no armour, a bow at the hip. He rode with spare horses and lived on their milk — the cheapest, fastest army on earth.\nWho chases a man who runs away? At the Kalka River in 1223 Mongol riders pretended to flee for nine days, lured the Russian princes after them, then turned and crushed them. The captured princes were laid under boards while the Mongol generals feasted on top.\n“A fleeing rabbit, or a wolf turning round. It all depended on how many days the princes kept chasing.”',
  steppeMedium: 'A Mongol horseman in hardened leather plates over his robe, lacquered against the rain. The Mongols also took every engineer they conquered, and used Chinese siege machines on Persian walls — the best tech, collected as loot.\nWhat was lost wherever he rode? In 1258 they took Baghdad in two weeks. The great library went into the river, which ran black with ink, it was said, and the last caliph was trampled by horses.\n“A conqueror of the world, or one more rider lost in it. It all depended on whether the Great Khan was still alive back home.”',
  kheshig: 'The Kheshig, Genghis Khan’s personal guard: gilded scale armour, a pointed helmet with an iron face mask, a sabre and a bow. They were sons of powerful families, guarding the Khan’s tent day and night — and doubling as hostages for their fathers’ good behaviour.\nYet what is a guard’s last duty? When Genghis died in 1227, his escort supposedly killed everyone they met on the road home, so nobody could tell where he was buried. Nobody has found the grave yet.\n“The Khan’s sworn shield, or a hostage for his father. It all depended on whether his family stayed loyal.”',
  cumanHeavy: 'A Cuman of a khan’s retinue: a mail shirt with rows of steel plates laced over it, a pointed helmet, and a steel face with a moustache hinged over his own. The Rus’ called them Polovtsy; they raided Kyiv, married its princes’ daughters and sold its captives in Crimea — neighbours, in-laws and slavers at once.\nYet who do you trust when the Mongols come? In 1239 Khan Köten led forty thousand families into Hungary and promised to be baptised for the privilege. In 1241, with the Mongols at the passes, a mob in Pest decided he was their spy and killed him. His Cumans rode south burning as they went, and the Hungarian army was destroyed at Mohi without them.\n“The kingdom’s new shield, or the Mongols’ spy. It all depended on what the mob in Pest believed.”',
  xiongnu: 'A Xiongnu horseman: a coat of felt or leather, a fur hat, a bow and a bronze knife. The northern nomads were the reason the first Chinese emperors started building long walls.\nYet how did their ruler guarantee obedience? Modu trained his archers to shoot wherever his whistling arrow flew: at his horse, then his wife, and finally his own father. Anyone who hesitated was beheaded. Then he made the Han emperor pay him tribute in silk and princesses.\n“A loyal archer, or a headless one. It all depended on how fast he shot at the whistling arrow.”',
  incaNoble: 'An Inca lord: a woven tunic covered in square patterns, gold plugs in his stretched earlobes and a braided cord round his head. The patterns may be a lost writing.\nWhat did loyalty buy at Cajamarca? In 1532 the emperor Atahualpa came to meet the Spanish, carried by his nobles. When the Spanish guns fired, the nobles held up the litter until they were cut down. Atahualpa paid a roomful of gold in ransom. They strangled him anyway.\n“A lord of the empire, or a hand under a falling litter. It all depended on whether Atahualpa trusted the invitation.”',
  incaUncu: 'An Inca warrior: a cotton tunic, a braided headband, quilted cotton armour, a sling and a star-shaped stone mace. He served the emperor as a labour tax, paid in work and, when needed, in blood.\nWho inherited the tax? The Spanish kept it after the Inca fell, and used it to send men into the silver mines of Potosí. So many never came out that it was called the mountain that eats men. The silver paid for Spain’s wars in Europe.\n“A warrior of the Inca, or a miner of Potosí. It all depended on whose census found his village.”',
  mexicaWarrior: 'An Aztec warrior in quilted cotton armour stiffened with salt water, with a wooden club edged in obsidian. Obsidian is sharper than steel and shatters like glass — stone against iron.\nWhat if the enemy plays by different rules? He fought to capture, not to kill, because the gods wanted living prisoners. The Spanish just fought to kill, and smallpox, arriving ahead of them in 1520, had killed half the city before the siege began.\n“A taker of captives, or a victim of the pox. It all depended on whether the sickness reached his house before the Spanish did.”',
};

